# Checked-in DERIVED artifacts — regenerated from source, never hand-edited.
#
#   engine-wasm/pkg/**        wasm-pack output rebuilt from engine-core by
#                             `make console-wasm` (both `lean` and `readmodel`
#                             subpath variants).
#   processos/assets/ir.gbnf  emitted by `cd processos && cargo run -- emit-gbnf`.
#
# Two consequences for each:
#
#  1. linguist-generated — collapse them in GitHub PR diffs (the compiled `.wasm`
#     are binary; the surrounding JS/TS/grammar are machine-emitted), so a review
#     sees the source change, not the regenerated blob.
#
#  2. merge=keep-ours — never attempt a three-way merge of the artifact. Two
#     branches that both rebuilt it otherwise collide on every integration (the
#     recurring conflict class behind Magikcraft/nano-bpm#1166, where the SOURCE
#     merged cleanly and only the committed `*_bg.wasm` blobs conflicted). The
#     authoritative bytes are not merged — they are REGENERATED against the merged
#     source. Each artifact then has its own CI drift guard:
#
#       * processos/assets/ir.gbnf — the processos test suite regenerates the
#         grammar in memory and asserts it equals the checked-in file (the
#         `checked_in_grammar_matches_emitter` parity test compares `emit_gbnf()`
#         against the `include_str!`'d bytes), so a stale commit fails the build
#         (a true byte-level content guard).
#
#       * engine-wasm/pkg — CI does NOT byte-diff the regenerated pkg (wasm-pack
#         output is not guaranteed byte-reproducible across environments, so a
#         `git diff --exit-code` would be a flaky guard). Instead the guard is
#         staleness-by-version: `engine-wasm (release drift guard)` fails the
#         build when the wasm inputs (engine-core/**, engine-wasm/src/**,
#         Cargo.toml/lock, pkg.package.json) have moved since the last release
#         tag but no version bump past it is pending, AND requires the committed
#         pkg/package.json to match the pkg.package.json template. The
#         `engine-wasm-ffi` job additionally rebuilds the wasm and runs the
#         committed package's test suites. The authoritative bytes are
#         regenerated by `make console-wasm` from the merged source before a
#         release (`make release-engine-wasm` refuses to tag until the built
#         pkg version matches the template).
#
#     Keeping "ours" on merge is therefore safe: the artifact is re-derived from
#     the merged source, and a version-stale engine-wasm build or a stale
#     ir.gbnf fails CI rather than shipping.
#
# The `keep-ours` driver is registered by `make setup` (`git config
# merge.keep-ours.driver true`). Git silently ignores an unregistered driver and
# falls back to today's manual binary-conflict resolve, so this degrades
# gracefully in a clone that has not run setup.
engine-wasm/pkg/**            linguist-generated=true merge=keep-ours
engine-wasm/pkg/**/*_bg.wasm  -diff
processos/assets/ir.gbnf      linguist-generated=true merge=keep-ours
